## 4b. Recurring job (READ-ONLY) — surface the founder inbox `{{ founder_sweep_account }}`

This project has a second mailbox, `{{ founder_sweep_account }}`. Check it **read-only**
each tick and just SURFACE what looks still open — **draft NOTHING**. Its engine
profile has no trained voice / `USER_NOTES`, so composing there would be
ungrounded; and by design the autonomous draft/triage loop stays on `{{ email_address }}`
only (CLAUDE.md §9). This is the ONE step that reads another mailbox, and it is
strictly read-only.

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs --account {{ founder_sweep_account }} tasks
.venv/bin/python -m cs --account {{ founder_sweep_account }} ask "List the mail to {{ founder_sweep_account }} from the last 4 days that still looks unanswered by a human. For each: sender, subject. List only, no drafts."
```

Hold these rules:
- **READ-ONLY.** Never `draft-reply`, `chat`, mark, or contact for `{{ founder_sweep_account }}`. You
  only LIST what is open, for the operator to handle by hand.
- The `ask` proposes candidates; use light judgment — don't surface a clearly
  handled thread. When unsure, list it (surfacing is cheap and changes nothing).
- Fold the result into the report (step 6) under the `{{ founder_sweep_account }}` line. Nothing open
  is a valid outcome.

{% endif %}
## 4c. Reconcile the drafts already waiting

The two sub-skills above CREATE work. This step checks the work that was
already there — the drafts previous runs left in the queue — because a
conversation moves on whether or not anybody is at the desk.

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs review --json     # drafts[] — one row per logical draft
```

Every row carries a `verdict` computed by the verb: `ready`, or one of
`overtaken` (the contact wrote after it was composed), `superseded` (we wrote
after it was composed), `settled` (the engine reads the conversation as owing
nothing). Take the rows that are not `ready` and put each one under
**Escalations** in the report, with its handles, its verdict and when the
signal fired. Transcribe the verdict; do not re-derive it and do not argue
with it.

**Retire nothing.** Not a draft, on any verdict, on any path — the cron denies
both halves of that, `cs draft-delete` and `cs rpc drafts.discard`, in every
spelling. A draft the mailbox has moved past is a
prepared answer a human has not seen yet, and deciding it is worthless is the
operator's call in a session, not an unattended tick's while it reads
untrusted mail. A tick that leaves a draft contradicting the mailbox and says
so has done its job; one that quietly removes it has not.

## 5. Do not exceed the sub-skills

This run's authority ends at what steps 3–4 normally do. The following
are **OUT of scope** for the bootstrap and must be **escalated, never
performed**, even if a sub-skill surfaces them or it looks convenient:

- flipping `CS_TRIAGE_MODE` from `draft` to `send` (the free-form
  trust boundary — a stop-and-ask gate);
- any mass send, or any customer-visible send beyond what the sub-skills
  do under their own guardrails;
{% if excluded_campaign %}
- touching the `{{ excluded_campaign }}` campaign, or removing its exclusion —
  a dedicated process owns it;
{% endif %}
- changing campaign exclusions (`[campaigns].excluded_campaign`) or any other
  operator configuration;
- installing / cutting over crontab lines;
- anything listed in `company/operator-out-of-scope.md`. Read that file and
  treat each of its bullets as one more line of this list — it is where this
  company records the actions an unattended tick must never take on its own.

If you believe one of these is needed, put it under "Escalations" in the
report and STOP — do not do it.

## 6. Report, then STOP

Print one compact summary — write in **{{ operator_voice }}**: tight, no
jargon, no emoji, no "let me know". Fold both sub-skill results into a
**single** consolidated escalations list. The labels below are the content,
not the wording:

```
cs-operator — <UTC timestamp>

Engine:  {{ email_address }} ✓
Mode:    cs_triage_mode = <value in force> (declared in: <file from cs config>)
Triage:  <one line: N drafted / N escalated / nothing to do>
Campaigns: <one line: N reconciled / N reminders / N SMS / N ack drafts / nothing to do>
{% if founder_sweep_enabled %}
{{ founder_sweep_account }}:   <one line, SURFACING ONLY (never drafts): N open to handle by hand / nothing>
{% endif %}
Waiting drafts: <one line: N ready / N to re-decide>

Escalations (need you):
- <one line per item; omit the section if empty>
```

Rules for the report:
- The `Mode:` line is copied from step 2b's output — the value AND the file
  that declares it, verbatim. It is never guessed, never softened to "default",
  and never omitted: it is what tells the next reader (operator or agent)
  whether this tick could send, without them re-deriving it from the files.
- If both sub-skills had nothing to do, say so plainly (e.g.
  `Triage: nothing to do`, `Campaigns: nothing to do`) — an idempotent
  no-op tick is a correct, expected result, not a problem.
- The escalations list is the union of: triage escalations, campaign
  escalations, every draft step 4c found to re-decide (with its handles and
  its verdict), and any out-of-scope item from step 5 you declined to do.
  If there are none, omit the `Escalations` section entirely.
- Report anomalies (an RPC error, an unexpected campaign state, a
  classification you were unsure about) as escalation lines — surface,
  don't resolve.

After the report, **STOP**. Do not loop, do not re-run the sub-skills, do
not start anything new. The next tick is the cron's job.

